# The Secret of Willow Tree

Aelric Moonshadow walked through the crowded hallway of St. Joseph's College, trying his best to look normal. This was not easy, because Aelric was not a normal boy. Underneath his school uniform and his cap, he was hiding pointed ears and hair that had a slight green colour when the sunlight touched it.

"Hey Aelric! Wait up!" His classmate Marcus ran to catch up with him. "Did you finish the maths homework? Question 5 was so difficult, I wanted to cry."

Aelric smiled politely. "Yes, it was quite challenging. The answer is 47.2 for the last part."

Marcus stared at him. "How you know that? You didn't even open your notebook!"

What Marcus didn't know was that Aelric had lived for over two hundred years in the ancient forests before his family moved to the city. To an elf who had studied the movements of stars and the patterns of nature for centuries, secondary school mathematics was like child's play.

During lunch time, Aelric sat alone under the old willow tree in the school garden. This was his favourite place, because the tree reminded him of home. He closed his eyes and whispered to it in the old elvish language. The tree's leaves rustled softly, as if answering him.

"You talk to trees?" A voice suddenly said. Aelric opened his eyes in shock. A girl named Sophie from his English class was standing there, holding her lunchbox.

"I... I was just... reciting poem," Aelric stammered. His English was perfect, but lying made him nervous.

Sophie sat down next to him anyway. "I like sitting here too. The other students think it's weird, but I feel like this place is... magical somehow."

Aelric's heart beat faster. Did she know? Could she sense it?

The biggest challenge came during the school camping trip in autumn. The teacher, Mr. Chan, announced that they would hike through the country park and sleep in tents for two nights.

"Perfect," Aelric thought nervously. "Being close to nature is exactly what I must avoid."

But the opposite happened. When they entered the forest, Aelric felt his powers growing stronger. Flowers seemed to turn towards him. A deer appeared from nowhere and walked beside him for ten minutes, which made all his classmates very confused and a little bit scared.

"Aelric, why is that deer following you?" Marcus whispered.

"Maybe I smell like food?" Aelric offered weakly.

On the second night, disaster happened. A typhoon warning came suddenly, and heavy rain flooded their campsite. One of the younger students, a small boy called Timothy, had wandered off to find his dropped water bottle and got lost in the dark forest.

While the teachers called the rescue services, Aelric slipped away quietly into the storm. In the forest, he didn't need to hide anymore. His elvish eyes could see clearly in the darkness, and he could hear Timothy's crying from half a kilometre away. The trees themselves guided him, their branches pointing the direction like green fingers.

He found the boy huddled under a rock, wet and shivering. "Don't be afraid," Aelric said gently, lifting him up. "I will bring you home."

When he carried Timothy back to the camp, everyone cheered. The teachers asked how he found the boy in such terrible darkness, but Aelric only shrugged and said, "I have very good eyesight."

From that day, something changed. Sophie looked at him differently, with a knowing smile. One afternoon under the willow tree, she said quietly, "You know, my grandmother told me stories about the old forest people. She said they still exist, hiding among us. She said you can recognise them because nature loves them."

Aelric held his breath.

"Don't worry," Sophie laughed. "Your secret is safe with me. But maybe... you could teach me that poem you were saying to the tree? It sounded beautiful."

Aelric smiled, and for the first time in many years, he felt that maybe, just maybe, he had found a real friend — someone who could see the elf, and still like the boy.

Under the willow tree, two students sat together, sharing secrets, while the leaves whispered their approval in the ancient language that only one of them could understand. [end of text]


